    culture: "San Jose's birth community blends Silicon Valley innovation with deep-rooted Vietnamese and Latino cultural traditions around pregnancy and postpartum care. You'll find doulas here who honor nằm cỡ (Vietnamese postpartum recovery) and cuarentena (Latino 40-day rest) alongside modern evidence-based birth prep. your cultural traditions are respected in the birth room." ,
    heroLocalDetail: "At 38 weeks, you're probably figuring out the fastest route from your place in Willow Glen or Berryessa to your delivery hospital. timing the 101 vs 280 commute. The Guadalupe River Park trail gives you flat, paved walking near downtown, and Alum Rock Park's shaded canyon is a quiet spot for those last peaceful walks before baby arrives." ,
    hospitalDetails: [
      { name: "Good Samaritan Hospital" , thumbnail: "/images/san-jose-ca-hospital-good-samaritan-hospital.webp" , address: "2425 Samaritan Drive, San Jose, CA 95124" , url: "https://www.dignityhealth.org/bay-area/locations/good-samaritan-hospital" , nicuLevel: "III" , doulaPolicy: "Doulas welcome as support persons" , medicaid: "Accepts Medi-Cal" , paragraph: "Good Samaritan Hospital in San Jose offers 16 spacious private birthing rooms with jetted tubs, birthing balls, and wireless fetal monitoring so you can move freely during labor. Their Level III NICU is staffed around the clock by neonatologists, with 24/7 live video streaming so you can stay connected to your newborn if specialized care is needed. Doulas are welcome as additional support persons alongside your partner, and the hospital supports skin-to-skin bonding and rooming-in. Lactation consultants and virtual breastfeeding support through Nest Collaborative are available after discharge. The hospital accepts Medi-Cal and offers maternity classes, childbirth education, and in-person tours. <a href='/birth-plan-template/'>Use our free hospital birth plan template</a> to prepare for your delivery here." },
      { name: "Regional Medical Center of San Jose" , thumbnail: "/images/san-jose-ca-hospital-regional-medical-center-of-san-jose.webp" , address: "225 N Jackson Ave, San Jose, CA 95116" , url: "https://www.regionalmedicalcenter.com" , nicuLevel: "III" , doulaPolicy: "Doulas welcome as support persons" , medicaid: "Accepts Medi-Cal" , paragraph: "Regional Medical Center of San Jose delivers nearly 1,000 babies each year with a team of obstetricians, neonatologists, and nurses providing personalized care. The Birth Center features 4 OB triage beds, 6 private birthing suites with sleeper chairs for partners, and 13 private postpartum suites in the Family Baby Unit. Their Level III NICU has 6 specialized beds for infants needing advanced intervention, with a BRIDGE Program for NICU-to-home transition support. The hospital is Baby-Friendly designated, offering delayed cord clamping, skin-to-skin bonding, and certified lactation support. Doulas are welcome as support persons, and the hospital accepts Medi-Cal. Multilingual staff speak Chinese, Farsi, Portuguese, Spanish, and Vietnamese with 24/7 interpreter services. <a href='/birth-plan-template/'>Use our free hospital birth plan template</a> to prepare for your delivery here." },
      { name: "Kaiser Permanente San Jose Medical Center" , thumbnail: "/images/san-jose-ca-hospital-kaiser-permanente-san-jose-medical-center.webp" , address: "250 Hospital Pkwy, San Jose, CA 95119" , url: "https://www.kp.org/santa-clara/sanjose" , nicuLevel: "III" , doulaPolicy: "Doulas welcome as support persons" , medicaid: "Accepts Medi-Cal" , paragraph: "Kaiser Permanente San Jose Medical Center offers a fully integrated maternity program with labor, delivery, and recovery in private rooms featuring built-in couches for your support person, free Wi-Fi, and TVs. Their Level III NICU and Intermediate Care Nursery provide specialized newborn care with neonatologists on-site. During labor you can have up to 4 guests including your partner and doula, with visiting hours from 8 a.m. to 8 p.m. The hospital supports skin-to-skin bonding, breastfeeding education, and a family-centered postpartum unit. Kaiser members can access contracted doula services at no extra cost through Kaiser's doula benefit for eligible plans, and the hospital accepts Medi-Cal managed care. A 24-hour delivery advice nurse is available before you come in. <a href='/birth-plan-template/'>Use our free hospital birth plan template</a> to prepare for your delivery here." }
    ],
    birthCenterDetails: [
      { name: "No birth centers in San Jose" , paragraph: "There are currently no freestanding birth centers operating within San Jose city limits. The Bay Area Birth Center in nearby Santa Clara or South Bay Midwifery may be options. or talk to your midwife about home birth if that's your preference." }
    ],
    medicaidNote: "Yes \u2014 California covers doula services through Medi-Cal \u2014 since January 2023, you can receive up to ~$1,587 in doula coverage including prenatal, birth, and postpartum visits. Ask your Medi-Cal managed care plan how to get started." ,
    insuranceNote: "If you have insurance through a Silicon Valley employer, check whether your plan includes doula benefits. many large tech-company plans now do. Medi-Cal doula coverage requires no prior authorization." ,
    faqs: [
      { q: "How much does a doula cost in San Jose?" , a: "San Jose doula services typically run $1,500–$3,000. Costs vary based on experience and package scope. many doulas in this area offer payment plans or sliding-scale options, so don't hesitate to ask." },
      { q: "Does California Medicaid cover doula services?" , a: "Yes. Medi-Cal has covered doula services since 2023, up to ~$1,587. Many San Jose doulas have started accepting Medi-Cal since the policy change." },
      { q: "What hospitals in San Jose have the highest level NICU?" , a: "Good Samaritan Hospital, Regional Medical Center, and Kaiser San Jose all offer Level III NICUs. For Level IV care, UCSF Benioff in San Francisco is the closest option." },
      { q: "Are there birth centers in San Jose?" , a: "There are no freestanding birth centers in San Jose proper right now. The Bay Area Birth Center in nearby Santa Clara or South Bay Midwifery are alternatives, or you can explore home birth with a licensed midwife." },
      { q: "Can I bring my doula to hospitals in San Jose?" , a: "Absolutely. Good Samaritan, Regional Medical Center, and Kaiser San Jose all allow doulas as support persons. Call ahead to your hospital's maternity triage to confirm current policies." },
      { q: "What postpartum resources are available in San Jose?" , a: "San Jose has solid postpartum support: lactation consultants at Santa Clara Valley Medical Center, postpartum groups through the YWCA Golden Gate Silicon Valley, Vietnamese postpartum meal services in East San Jose, and Santa Clara County's Perinatal Equity Initiative for Black moms." }
    ],
    midwifeInfo: {
      paragraph: "California licenses Certified Nurse-Midwives (CNMs) through the Board of Registered Nursing and Licensed Midwives (LMs) through the Medical Board of California. This means both hospital-based and out-of-hospital midwifery care are legally regulated and accessible in San Jose. Good Samaritan and Regional Medical Center have CNM-attended births, and several LMs serve the South Bay for home birth. If you are planning a home birth in San Jose, your midwife operates under a state-issued license." ,
      credentialTypes: " and LMs" ,
    },
